

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Good — that keeps the backend contract out of scope.

  Next: [1mhow long the identity survives, and where it lives.[0m This is a storage-format decision, and it's the one that's
  genuinely expensive to reverse — once IDs are sitting in users' browsers under a given key and shape, changing it
  means writing migration code for data you can't see.

  - [1m[38;5;153msessionStorage[0m — survives reloads and navigation, dies when the tab closes. Buys "other forms can read it" within a
    working session; costs a re-login per tab. No cross-tab sharing.
  - [1m[38;5;153mlocalStorage[0m — survives tab and browser close until explicitly cleared. Buys true "stay logged in"; costs a real
    logout path (otherwise the ID outlives the user's intent) and it's readable by any script on the origin, so an XSS
    bug hands over the identity. Shared across tabs.
  - [1mIn-memory module singleton[0m — a variable other code imports. Buys simplicity and leaks nothing to disk; costs: does
    not survive a reload, which I think fails your "persist" requirement.
  - [1mCookie[0m — persists [3mand[0m rides along on requests to the backend automatically. Buys server-side use for free; costs
    cookie attribute decisions ([38;5;153mSameSite[39m, [38;5;153mSecure[39m, expiry) and it's the wrong tool if only client JS reads it.

  [1mMy recommendation: [38;5;153msessionStorage[39m, with the accessor written so the backing store is swappable.[0m You said persist and
  cross-app, and [38;5;153msessionStorage[39m delivers both within a session without committing you to a "remember me" feature and its
  logout/expiry obligations before you've asked for one. If you later want browser-close survival, that's a one-line
  change behind the accessor — whereas starting at [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m means shipping a logout story now.
